+# Carrier Construction Yards, Supply Chains, Certification, and Commissioning Runtime
+
+## Runtime Purpose
+
+Make every large carrier family genuinely player-buildable while preserving the reality that civilization-scale construction depends on institutions, labor, yards, resources, standards, politics and time.
+
+> **The player may author the project. The ship is built by a civilization.**
+
+# 1. Construction Campaign Model
+
+A carrier project is a persistent program with linked workstreams.
+
+```text
+CarrierConstructionProgram
+├── need and mission case
+├── charter and stakeholders
+├── requirements baseline
+├── design lineage and architecture
+├── yard and orbital/site preparation
+├── finance and risk allocation
+├── material and component supply chains
+├── labor and training
+├── structural assembly
+├── systems integration
+├── habitat and ecological commissioning
+├── certification and trials
+├── population embarkation
+├── launch or departure
+└── lifecycle support
+```
+
+Each workstream can advance, stall, fail, fork or be renegotiated.
+
+# 2. Entry Paths
+
+Players should not always begin from a blank design.
+
+## 2.1 New public project
+
+A settlement, federation or route authority commissions a new carrier.
+
+## 2.2 Cooperative build
+
+Workers, residents and partner settlements pool capital, labor and future capacity.
+
+## 2.3 Private commission
+
+A wealthy character or firm funds construction under law, debt and resident obligations.
+
+## 2.4 Incomplete hull recovery
+
+The player acquires or is entrusted with a stalled project.
+
+## 2.5 Conversion
+
+A cargo vessel, military hull, habitat or station becomes a carrier through major refit.
+
+## 2.6 Distributed assembly
+
+Many regions manufacture modules later joined in orbit or at a route node.
+
+## 2.7 Grown or symbiotic project
+
+A living or adaptive carrier is cultivated through ecological, medical and diplomatic work rather than ordinary assembly.
+
+# 3. Requirements Phase
+
+The project must define:
+
+- intended population range;
+- mission emphasis vector;
+- common-core capacity;
+- operating regions;
+- acceleration and gravity strategy;
+- autonomy duration;
+- carried-base ecology;
+- dock and interface standards;
+- hazard environment;
+- repair doctrine;
+- governance and ownership;
+- design life;
+- expansion plan;
+- evacuation and rescue obligations;
+- affordability and lifecycle budget.
+
+Requirements may conflict.
+
+The player can facilitate, engineer, manipulate or expose these conflicts, but cannot make mass, heat, labor or politics disappear.
+
+# 4. Design Authority
+
+Design work may be performed by:
+
+- public engineering institutes;
+- player-founded design office;
+- cooperative guild;
+- corporation;
+- route house;
+- machine collective;
+- alien partner;
+- inherited standards body.
+
+Authorship records:
+
+- contributors;
+- intellectual and cultural provenance;
+- safety responsibility;
+- licensing;
+- restricted knowledge;
+- later modifications.
+
+A player can become principal architect without personally drawing every pipe.
+
+# 5. Yard Types
+
+## 5.1 Surface heavy yard
+
+Builds atmospheric or ocean-capable modules and launches them through heavy lift, orbital elevator or assembled carrier stages.
+
+## 5.2 Orbital dock
+
+Assembles hulls without planetary weight but depends on transport, workforce habitats and orbital safety.
+
+## 5.3 Asteroid foundry
+
+Uses local metal and shielding mass, reducing launch burden while creating remote logistics.
+
+## 5.4 Mobile yard carrier
+
+A multirole carrier with strong construction emphasis builds or refits other vessels while remaining a habitat and logistics center.
+
+## 5.5 Distributed cooperative yard
+
+Many small fabricators produce standardized sections. Integration risk and governance become central.
+
+## 5.6 Biological nursery
+
+Grows living hull tissues, microbial ecologies or symbiotic organs under negotiated agency.
+
+A project can combine yard types.
+
+# 6. Yard Capacity
+
+Yard capability includes:
+
+- maximum structural envelope;
+- lifting and positioning;
+- vacuum or pressure work;
+- clean integration spaces;
+- high-energy testing;
+- material processing;
+- precision metrology;
+- software and identity integration;
+- life-support ecology commissioning;
+- workforce habitation;
+- emergency response;
+- traffic and exclusion zones.
+
+Players may need to upgrade the yard before building the ship.
+
+# 7. Supply Chains
+
+Carrier construction requires categories such as:
+
+- structural metals, composites or biological tissues;
+- pressure membranes;
+- radiation shielding;
+- propellant tanks;
+- drives and power conversion;
+- radiators and thermal fluids;
+- sensors and communications;
+- control hardware;
+- docking systems;
+- atmosphere, water and waste equipment;
+- medical systems;
+- fabrication machinery;
+- agricultural and microbial cultures;
+- furniture, tools and ordinary domestic goods.
+
+The ordinary goods matter. A hull is not habitable because its reactor works.
+
+## 7.1 Supplier state
+
+Every major supply contract tracks:
+
+- producer;
+- standards;
+- delivery schedule;
+- quality evidence;
+- labor conditions;
+- transport route;
+- price and currency;
+- replacement guarantee;
+- geopolitical risk;
+- dependency on rare inputs.
+
+# 8. Labor and Skill
+
+Construction requires:
+
+- structural engineers;
+- welders, assemblers and fabricators;
+- electricians and thermal technicians;
+- software and control engineers;
+- life-support ecologists;
+- medical and accessibility reviewers;
+- logisticians;
+- inspectors;
+- pilots and tug crews;
+- safety teams;
+- educators and apprentices;
+- future residents.
+
+Player actions may include:
+
+- performing specialist work;
+- training apprentices;
+- resolving labor disputes;
+- improving tools;
+- redesigning unsafe access;
+- negotiating schedules;
+- investigating defects;
+- organizing worker ownership.
+
+# 9. Construction Stages
+
+## Stage 1: program formation
+
+Need, stakeholders, finance and governance.
+
+## Stage 2: yard readiness
+
+Site, tools, workforce and exclusions.
+
+## Stage 3: long-lead components
+
+Drives, power, pressure structures, radiators and docking systems.
+
+## Stage 4: structural assembly
+
+Load paths and major hull sections.
+
+## Stage 5: utilities
+
+Power, thermal, atmosphere, water, information and transit.
+
+## Stage 6: districts
+
+Homes, workspaces, care, gardens, commons, education and harbors.
+
+## Stage 7: integration
+
+Systems interact under realistic loads.
+
+## Stage 8: commissioning
+
+Cleaning, calibration, ecological startup, stocking and training.
+
+## Stage 9: trials
+
+Static, maneuvering, endurance, emergency and carried-base tests.
+
+## Stage 10: acceptance and departure
+
+Defects, title, debt, public obligations and initial mission are settled.
+
+# 10. Time and Compression
+
+A civilization carrier may take years or decades in-world.
+
+The game supports:
+
+- time acceleration;
+- character switching;
+- profession play at the yard;
+- remote management;
+- generational succession;
+- milestone ceremonies;
+- urgent side missions affecting the project;
+- progressive use of completed districts.
+
+Long time must create history rather than a passive timer.
+
+# 11. Finance
+
+Funding sources include:
+
+- public budgets;
+- cooperative shares;
+- bonds;
+- route-service contracts;
+- future transport rights;
+- philanthropic or religious endowments;
+- salvage proceeds;
+- private equity;
+- intersettlement treaties;
+- alien exchange agreements.
+
+Finance creates claims and obligations.
+
+The game must distinguish:
+
+- title;
+- debt;
+- voting rights;
+- service guarantees;
+- public trust restrictions;
+- creditor seizure rights;
+- resident protections.
+
+# 12. Certification
+
+Certification is evidence that a defined configuration is safe for a defined operating envelope.
+
+Required evidence may include:
+
+- structural proof;
+- pressure testing;
+- fire and atmosphere containment;
+- electrical isolation;
+- thermal balance;
+- propulsion and control;
+- docking compatibility;
+- software and cybersecurity;
+- medical and accessibility review;
+- evacuation time;
+- life-support endurance;
+- contamination handling;
+- carried-craft deployment.
+
+Certification may be:
+
+- recognized across many jurisdictions;
+- local only;
+- provisional;
+- expired;
+- fraudulent;
+- contested by another species or legal system.
+
+# 13. Trials
+
+Trials should be playable and dangerous without becoming arbitrary sabotage.
+
+Examples:
+
+- cold-start from dormant state;
+- maximum planned acceleration;
+- partial radiator loss;
+- district pressure isolation;
+- emergency docking;
+- evacuation drill;
+- contaminated craft arrival;
+- high-load fabrication;
+- long-duration habitat closure;
+- simultaneous deployment and casualty response;
+- command succession after simulated loss.
+
+Failed trials create defects, redesign, political conflict and schedule cost.
+
+# 14. Commissioning Population
+
+Future residents should participate before departure.
+
+They:
+
+- test homes;
+- establish schools and clinics;
+- seed gardens;
+- name districts;
+- create local rules;
+- discover inaccessible design errors;
+- decide what cultural objects travel;
+- negotiate work and governance.
+
+A carrier is not commissioned when the engines start. It is commissioned when its technical and civic systems can sustain real life.
+
+# 15. Player-Buildability Levels
+
+## Module builder
+
+The player designs or fabricates one module.
+
+## Systems integrator
+
+The player coordinates several connected modules.
+
+## Yard organizer
+
+The player expands construction capacity and supply networks.
+
+## Program founder
+
+The player creates the charter, financing and mission coalition.
+
+## Principal builder
+
+The player guides the overall design and construction campaign.
+
+## Inheritor
+
+The player receives an unfinished, damaged or politically troubled carrier project.
+
+All are valid “I helped build this ship” fantasies.
+
+# 16. Anti-Grind Rules
+
+- Do not require one player to deliver millions of identical material units manually.
+- Let infrastructure and institutions perform repeated throughput after being established.
+- Keep decisions, bottlenecks, exceptional work and consequences playable.
+- Compress familiar tasks.
+- Make quality, route, politics and timing matter more than repetitive clicking.
+- Never sell progress through artificial wait timers or monetized acceleration.
+
+# 17. Failure Outcomes
+
+A project may become:
+
+- completed as designed;
+- completed smaller;
+- delayed;
+- over budget;
+- politically captured;
+- converted to another mission;
+- split into several vessels;
+- abandoned and later recovered;
+- launched with known defects;
+- transformed into a station;
+- completed by a successor generation.
+
+Failure can create a valuable world artifact rather than deleting the project.
+
+## Runtime Maxim
+
+> **Building a carrier is not the act of collecting enough resources. It is the act of making thousands of dependencies trustworthy enough to leave home together.**
